<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">14 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 14 August, from 1040 to 1945.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1040</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King Duncan I of Scotland is killed by Macbeth</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Duncan I was killed in battle near Elgin by Macbeth, who took the throne. Macbeth ruled for 17 years, a far more capable king than the villain of Shakespeare's play written five centuries later.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1457</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Mainz Psalter becomes the first dated printed book</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Johann Fust and Peter Schöffer completed the Mainz Psalter, the first printed book to carry a date and printer's names. It also pioneered colour printing with its red and blue initials.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1900</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">International forces relieve the besieged legations in Beijing</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Troops of the Eight-Nation Alliance entered Beijing and lifted the 55-day siege of the foreign legations by Boxers and Qing troops. China was forced to pay a crushing indemnity.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1935</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Franklin Roosevelt signs the Social Security Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Roosevelt signed the Social Security Act, creating old-age pensions, unemployment insurance and aid for dependent children. It became the cornerstone of the American welfare state.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1945</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Japan accepts the Allied terms of surrender</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Japan's government notified the Allies that it accepted the Potsdam Declaration, and President Truman announced the news that evening. Emperor Hirohito recorded a broadcast to his people for the next day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a></div></li></ol>
